<h2 class="doc-h2">Snap Points</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The drag rests rather than only closing. The heights are ordinary CSS lengths, resolved by
    asking the browser what they mean, so <code>31rem 1</code> is a real size and a fraction of
    the viewport.
</p>
<Example Source="DrawerSnapPoints.razor"><DrawerSnapPoints /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Non Modal</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    <code>show()</code> instead of <code>showModal()</code>: no backdrop, no focus trap, the page
    behind stays usable. This page said it was impossible because a modal dialog is modal by
    definition — which is true, and is exactly why this calls the other method.
</p>
<Example Source="DrawerNonModal.razor"><DrawerNonModal /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Nested</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The top layer is a stack, not a single slot, so a drawer opened from inside another sits on
    top of it and the first stays put. This needed no code at all — only the check that the note
    claiming otherwise was wrong.
</p>
<Example Source="DrawerNested.razor"><DrawerNested /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Responsive</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    shadcn's version renders a Dialog on a desktop and a Drawer on a phone, chosen in React from a
    media query. Static SSR has no such hook and needs none: both are the same
    <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, so one element with a media query on its frame is the whole thing.
</p>
<Example Source="DrawerResponsive.razor"><DrawerResponsive /></Example>

